      <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-foreground mb-6">The Ones Who Didn't Make It</h2>
      <div class="text-sm md:text-base text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-5 max-w-3xl">
        <p>This is the part nobody wants to talk about. Some of the dogs and pigeons we found were already past the point of saving — injuries too severe, bodies too broken. The vet would look up and shake their head. There was nothing left to fix.</p>
        <p>But that was never the same as nothing left to do. We gave them palliative care — warmth, food, medication for pain, and presence. Someone sitting beside them so they didn't have to die alone. For days, sometimes weeks, until the end came on its own terms.</p>
        <p>We handled each burial ourselves. In the same community the dog had lived in. With care. With dignity. Because the true measure of compassion isn't what you do for the ones you can save — it's how you treat the ones you can't.</p>
        <p class="text-foreground/50 italic">Every life — no matter how short — deserves to end with someone who cared.</p>
      </div>
